.TH smi-serve-files "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Server Command"
.SH NAME
smi-serve-files \- Serve many web roots over HTTP by host name, behind a reverse proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-serve-files
.RI [ CONFIG_FILE ]
.RI [ PORT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Serves static files over HTTP with an Elixir server, for
.BR haproxy (1)
or
.BR nginx (8)
to sit in front of.
One port serves many web roots: the
.B Host
header of each request selects the web root, the way an nginx virtual host does.
Binds to
.B 127.0.0.1
only.
Runs in the foreground until killed.
.PP
The host to web root mapping is read from
.IR CONFIG_FILE ,
one mapping per line:
.PP
.RS
.I hostname   directory
.RE
.PP
The host name and an absolute directory, separated by whitespace.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
A request for an unmapped host gets
.BR 404 .
.PP
The directory is the host's root and holds two folders:
.IR brand ,
served at
.BR / ,
and
.IR apps ,
served at
.BR /apps/ .
So
.I DIRECTORY/brand/style.css
is
.BR https://host/style.css ,
and
.I DIRECTORY/apps/shop/main.js
is
.BR https://host/apps/shop/main.js .
One host therefore serves one brand page and any number of applications, each application a folder
under
.IR apps ,
the same layout
.BR nginx (8)
serves from
.IR /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/ .
.PP
Each host has a second, unreleased copy of that layout in
.IR DIRECTORY/draft/ ,
with its own
.I brand
and
.I apps
folders.
A request is served from it, at the very same paths, when the proxy sends
.PP
.RS
.B X-Smi-Variant: draft
.RE
.PP
which the proxy does for a request carrying a valid preview cookie, and never on a client's say-so:
the proxy deletes the header before it sets its own.
A new build is copied to
.I draft
first, checked in place, and copied to the live folders once it is accepted.
A host without a
.I draft
folder answers
.B 404
for such requests, so the header is harmless where no draft exists.
The host is taken from
.BR X-Forwarded-Host ,
when the proxy sets it, otherwise from the
.B Host
header; the port and letter case are ignored.
The proxy must pass the client's host through, for example nginx
.B "proxy_set_header Host $host"
or haproxy's default.
.PP
Must be started as root.
For every directory in the config it sets the tree to
.B root:root
with mode
.B 644
for files and
.B 755
for directories, then drops to the
.B microservice
user for the server itself.
The service user can therefore read every site but modify none of them, so a compromise of the
server cannot rewrite the served files of any host.
.PP
There is one server working directory,
.IR $(smi-var-location)/smi-serve-files/workingdir ,
owned by
.B microservice
with mode
.BR 700 .
It is the service user's
.B HOME
and the only place it may write: the
.B Mix.install
dependency cache and runtime state.
The first run needs network access to fetch the dependencies; later runs use the cache.
.PP
A request that matches no file is answered with an
.IR index.html ,
so a single page application resolves its own routes: for a path under
.BR /apps/ APP /
it is
.IR DIRECTORY/apps/APP/index.html ,
for every other path
.IR DIRECTORY/brand/index.html ,
and below
.I DIRECTORY/draft
for a draft request.
If that file is missing, the request gets
.BR 404 .
.PP
Everything under a served directory is public.
Only
.B GET
and
.B HEAD
are accepted; any other method gets
.BR 405 .
HTTP/2 and WebSocket are disabled.
A request gets
.B 404
without touching the file system when its path is longer than 2048 bytes, deeper than 16
directories, or has a component that, once percent-decoded, is empty, starts with
.BR . ,
or contains a slash, backslash, colon, control or non-printable character.
Any served directory that contains something other than a regular file or a directory, a symbolic
link in particular, stops the command from starting, since it could reach outside the tree.
.PP
Every request is written to standard output in the
.B combined
log format of
.BR nginx (8)
with the served host name as a leading field, one line per request.
The client address is the
.B last
.B X-Forwarded-For
entry, which is the one the proxy appended, so a client cannot forge it by sending its own.
Header values in the log have quotes and non-printable characters replaced by
.BR ? .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I CONFIG_FILE
The host to web root mapping.
Defaults to
.IR $(smi-etc-location)/smi-serve-files.config .
.TP
.I PORT
Port to listen on, 1 to 65535.
Defaults to
.BR 8140 .
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
The mapping is a plain text file rather than Elixir or Erlang config, because the server is a
single
.B .exs
script, not a Mix release: it has no
.I sys.config
or
.I runtime.exs
to read, and a data file that both the shell wrapper and the script can parse keeps the security
side, the directory permissions, and the routing side reading the same source of truth.
A line is a host name, whitespace, an absolute directory.
An example, shipped commented out:
.PP
.RS
.B APPS=$(smi-applications-location ee has)
.br
app1.example.com   /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/app1.example.com
.br
app2.example.com   /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/app2.example.com
.RE
.PP
A directory is validated before it is served: it must be absolute, must not be
.BR / ,
must exist, and must contain only regular files and directories.
It is then set to
.BR root:root .
.PP
The last line counts even when the file does not end with a newline.
A directory name must not contain whitespace, because the server reads the rest of the line as
the directory while the shell wrapper reads one field, and the two would then secure and serve
different directories; such a line is refused.
The same host name twice is refused as well: the server would keep only the last mapping while
the wrapper had already secured both directories.
.SH REVERSE PROXY
.PP
The server listens on
.B 127.0.0.1:8140
only, so it is never reached directly: a reverse proxy on the public interface forwards to it and
must pass the client's host through unchanged, since the host is what selects the web root.
One backend serves every tenant; the proxy only has to forward the hosts to it.
.PP
.BR haproxy (1)
is the proxy this server is meant for.
It terminates TLS on
.BR 8091 ,
answers
.B 8090
with a redirect to HTTPS, and forwards to this backend.
It passes the original
.B Host
by default, so a backend pointing at
.B 127.0.0.1:8140
needs no host rewriting; it also sets
.B X-Forwarded-Host
and appends the client to
.BR X-Forwarded-For ,
whose last entry is what this server logs.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
backend files
    http-request set-header X-Forwarded-Proto https
    http-request set-header X-Forwarded-Host %[req.hdr(host),host_only,lower]

    server files 127.0.0.1:8140 maxconn 512

frontend web_https
    acl draft req.cook(draft) -m str -f /etc/haproxy/vhosts/draft-tokens.lst
    http-request del-header X-Smi-Variant
    http-request set-header X-Smi-Variant draft if draft
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
.BR nginx (8)
is the other front in the same setup, on
.B 8080
and
.BR 8081 ,
but it serves the very same folders itself instead of proxying, and is deliberately
.B not
allowed to connect to
.BR 8140 .
Whichever front is public is decided by the firewall, which forwards 80 and 443 to one pair of
ports or the other.
.PP
On an SELinux enforcing system the proxy may not open a connection to this port until the port is
labelled, and a denied connect shows as
.B "503 Service Unavailable"
in haproxy with no request reaching this server.
The port labels, the policy modules and the firewall rules are documented once, in the
configuration repository:
.IR configurations/nginx/SELINUX-GUIDE.md ,
.IR configurations/nginx/FIREWALL-GUIDE.md .
Do not turn on
.B haproxy_connect_any
or
.BR httpd_can_network_connect :
the guides label the single port instead.
.SH EXAMPLE PAGE
.PP
The toolset ships one static page,
.BR hello-server ,
to check that serving works before pointing this at a real application.
It is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no backend calls: it renders a greeting, and its script
replaces one line of text, so a correct render means the HTML, the stylesheet, the script and an
.I assets/
file were all served.
.PP
The default config ships two ready to test mappings,
.B tenant1.test
and
.BR tenant2.test ,
pointing at web roots under
.IR $(smi-applications-location\ ee\ has) .
Fill both web roots with a copy of the example page and start the server:
.PP
.RS
.B APPS=$(smi-applications-location ee has)
.br
.B sudo mkdir -p ${APPS}/tenant1/www ${APPS}/tenant2/www
.br
.B sudo cp -R $(smi-lib-location)/hello-server/. ${APPS}/tenant1/www
.br
.B sudo cp -R $(smi-lib-location)/hello-server/. ${APPS}/tenant2/www
.br
.B sudo env PATH="$PATH" smi-serve-files
.RE
.PP
The
.B /.
at the end of the source is required, and is the whole difference between
.I www/index.html
and
.IR www/hello-server/index.html .
Without it
.B cp -R
copies the directory
.I into
.I www
whenever
.I www
already exists.
With it the
.I contents
are copied, whether
.I www
exists or not, so the command can be repeated to update the site.
.PP
The server binds to
.B 127.0.0.1
and routes by host, so a test request must carry the host name.
With
.BR curl (1)
that is the
.B Host
header; from a browser add
.B tenant1.test
and
.B tenant2.test
to
.I /etc/hosts
pointing at
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
.PP
.RS
.B curl -s -H 'Host: tenant1.test' http://127.0.0.1:8140/
.br
.B curl -s -H 'Host: tenant2.test' http://127.0.0.1:8140/
.br
.B curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' -H 'Host: unknown.test' http://127.0.0.1:8140/
.RE
.PP
The first two print the served
.IR index.html ;
the third prints
.BR 404 ,
the answer for an unmapped host.
.PP
.B env PATH="$PATH"
is what lets root find the toolset at all.
.I /opt/setmy.info/bin
is put on
.B PATH
by
.IR /etc/profile.d/setmy-info.sh ,
which root under
.B sudo
has not read.
On a distribution whose sudoers sets
.BR secure_path ,
Red Hat family ones do by default,
.B sudo
uses that path to look the command up and plain
.B sudo smi-serve-files
fails with
.BR "command not found" .
.B env
works because it is itself on
.BR secure_path ,
so
.B sudo
finds
.BR env ,
which then resolves
.B smi-serve-files
using the
.B PATH
it was given.
The alternative is to add
.I /opt/setmy.info/bin
to
.B secure_path
in
.IR /etc/sudoers .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $(smi-etc-location)/smi-serve-files.config
The host to web root mapping.
.TP
.I $(smi-var-location)/smi-serve-files/workingdir
Service user's home, dependency cache and runtime state.
Never served.
.TP
.I $(smi-lib-location)/hello-server
The example page.
.TP
.I $(smi-lib-location)/elixir/smi-serve-files.exs
The server.
.TP
.IR /opt/erlang ,\  /opt/elixir
The runtime.
Both are put first on the
.B PATH
given to the service user, so the server runs on the toolset's own Erlang and Elixir whatever
else is on the caller's path.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 1
Bad arguments, not run as root, missing
.B microservice
user, missing config file, no host mappings, a bad host name, or a served directory that is
missing, not absolute, is
.BR / ,
or holds something other than files and directories.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.B sudo env PATH="$PATH" smi-serve-files
.br
.B sudo env PATH="$PATH" smi-serve-files /opt/setmy.info/etc/smi-serve-files.config 8081
.RE
